Fix: timed multi-object waits sleep instead of spinning (Linux / POSIX)

Applies to: rexglue-sdk nightly-20260921-923c1a59 (thirdparty/rexglue-sdk)
Apply with: git -C thirdparty/rexglue-sdk apply ../../patches/rexglue-sdk/0022-timed-multi-wait-no-spin.patch
Status:     upstream CPU-waste bug (report to rexglue-sdk)

WHAT BROKE
  The SDK's "Audio Worker" thread used ~49% of a CPU core at any frame rate,
  even with no sound client registered to it (the port plays sound through
  its own output since findings/54, so the worker only waits).

WHY
  PosixConditionBase::WaitMultiple (src/core/threading_posix.cpp) polls its
  handles and sleeps min(remaining, 1 ms) between polls, but `remaining` was
  cast DOWN to whole milliseconds. Alertable waits call it in 1 ms slices
  (kAlertablePollSlice), so right after the start remaining = 0.99 ms -> 0 ms
  -> sleep_for(0) returns at once -> the loop spun (trylock every handle,
  allocate a vector, read the clock) for the whole slice. The Audio Worker's
  WaitAny(..., alertable, 500 ms) then spun for 500 ms, slept 500 ms (no
  client) and repeated: half a core. ComputeAlertableWaitTimeout had the same
  cut: in the last part of a millisecond before a deadline it asked for a
  0 ms wait, so the alertable loop spun until the deadline.

FIX
  WaitMultiple sleeps min(remaining in MICROseconds, 1000 us);
  ComputeAlertableWaitTimeout rounds the remaining time UP (std::chrono::ceil).
  A wait may now end up to ~1 ms after its deadline instead of spinning to it.

HOW FOUND
  Per-thread CPU from /proc/<pid>/task/*/stat (Audio Worker 49.3%), then the
  port's sampler on that thread (console `profile 5 <file> Audio Worker`):
  every sample inside AudioSystem::WorkerThreadMain -> WaitMultiple /
  pthread_mutex_trylock / clock_gettime. After the fix: 0.2% (port's sound
  output) and 0.5% (SDK sound output, --sound_out_native=false), 0 gaps.

diff --git a/src/core/threading_posix.cpp b/src/core/threading_posix.cpp
index 9ac8d15..b19b179 100644
--- a/src/core/threading_posix.cpp
+++ b/src/core/threading_posix.cpp
@@ -414,8 +414,14 @@ class PosixConditionBase {
       if (timeout == std::chrono::milliseconds::max()) {
         std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(1));
       } else {
-        auto remaining = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(end_time - now);
-        auto sleep_time = std::min(remaining, std::chrono::milliseconds(1));
+        // Remaining time in MICROseconds: cut down to whole milliseconds it
+        // was 0 for the whole of a 1 ms wait (end - now = 0.99 ms -> 0 ms),
+        // so sleep_for(0) returned at once and this loop SPUN until the
+        // deadline. Alertable waits ask in 1 ms slices, so every alertable
+        // multi-wait with a timeout (the SDK's Audio Worker: ~49% of a core)
+        // burnt a core while it waited.
+        auto remaining = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(end_time - now);
+        auto sleep_time = std::min(remaining, std::chrono::microseconds(1000));
         std::this_thread::sleep_for(sleep_time);
       }
     }
@@ -1106,7 +1112,9 @@ std::chrono::milliseconds ComputeAlertableWaitTimeout(
   if (deadline == std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point::max()) {
     return kAlertablePollSlice;
   }
-  auto remaining = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(
+  // Rounded UP: cut down, the last part of a millisecond before the deadline
+  // became a 0 ms wait = the caller's loop spun until the deadline.
+  auto remaining = std::chrono::ceil<std::chrono::milliseconds>(
       deadline - std::chrono::steady_clock::now());
   if (remaining <= std::chrono::milliseconds::zero()) {
     return std::chrono::milliseconds::zero();
